\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  finds ringgit defence tricky. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Reme Ahmad \par \uc2 685 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 1998\par 06:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, Jan 11 (Reuters) - Defending the Malaysian ringgit is proving tricky for the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it walks a fine line between supporting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and strangling the economy, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is using the twin tools of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rates to defend the ringgit, but Asia's economic gloom has made that job harder than usual. \par \par \uc2  Last week the ringgit slid 13 percent, forcing Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to nudge up interbank rates and repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy ringgit and sell dollars, traders aid. \par \par \uc2  But in protecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must tread between not depleting the country's reserves and not squeezing the economy with higher interest rates, the analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "At this point of time, they can't afford to hike interest rates too much because equities and the property market are already weak," said Jimmy Koh, regional economist at financial market consultant I.D.E.A. \par \par \uc2  "And you can sell dollar/ringgit, but how much can you sell?" he added. \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably opt to keep rates firm to encourage savings while not choking off the economy. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, Bank Negara tried, but failed, to prevent the ringgit from falling below 4.0 a dollar. \par \par \uc2  It then spent the rest of last week successfully defending the ringgit from falling to 5.0 as Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  came under fire. \par \par \uc2  "It's not a question of a wise move or not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but a question of integrity. Bank Negara can't let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken as far as people like," said a spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a European bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit ended Kuala Lumpur trading on Friday at 4.5700 a dollar, down 13.4 percent from 3.96 only a week ago. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit has lost more than 40 percent of its value against the dollar since early July when the Asian economic crisis first erupted. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said stoutly defending the ringgit as a matter of policy would ultimately fail and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  knew this. \par \par \uc2  "If they are providing liquidity to the market it's okay. But if you're changing your policy then it's wasting money. They need all the money they can get," said Desmond Supple, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at BZW. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Bank Negara may have spent around $1 billion in its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. \par \par \uc2  Last Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said its international reserves stood at $21.71 billion as at December 31 last year, before the January \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers speculated that to prevent the reserves from depleting, part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds could have come from state oil company Petroliam Nasional Bhd (Petronas). \par \par \uc2  Petronas, the country's most profitable company, earns its oil revenue in dollars and was rumoured to have converted these to ringgit in large amounts in the recent past to stop drastic slides in the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  No confirmation from Petronas could be obtained on the speculation. \par \par \uc2  On another battlefront, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been nudging up interbank rates to make the ringgit more attractive to hold, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Concerns over higher inflation as the price of imported goods jump with the declining ringgit were also a reason for allowing rates to creep higher. \par \par \uc2  "The perception is that we may see strong inflation, so Bank Negara may be willing to let rates rise," said a futures dealer at a merchant bank. \par \par \uc2  Futures contracts on the benchmark three-month Kuala Lumpur Interbank Offered Rate (KLIBOR) slid to record lows last week as players expect firmer rates in coming months. \par \par \uc2  The futures contracts move in opposite direction to expectations of interest rate movements. \par \par \uc2  The key March KLIBOR futures contract ended Friday trading at 88.70 for an implied yield of 11.3 percent, against 89.50 or an implied yield of 10.5 percent only a week ago. \par \par \uc2  A third method of defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is through capital controls, but Malaysian leaders have emphasised they will adhere to a flexible exchange rate policy. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1b09a40\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
