\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia Ringgit Down Early Vs U.S. Dlr On Corporate Demand \b0\par\par\uc2 306 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 1998\par 03:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR (Dow Jones)--The Malaysian ringgit is lower against the U.S. dollar in early trading Friday as commercial demand for the greenback continues to pick up on fears of further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciations across the region, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0204 GMT (9:04 p.m. EST), the dollar was quoted at MYR4.7739, up from MYR4.6800 late Thursday. The dollar has so far traded between MYR4.6000 and MYR4.7739 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad's comments on the state of the economy, televised live, failed to shore up confidence in the market. He said the current economic slowdown would be more difficult to reverse than the recession Malaysia faced in the mid-1980s because of the severe depreciation of the ringgit. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost around 45% of its value since the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis began in July. \par \par \uc2  'The PM's comments didn't have much effect one way or the other,' one trader said. 'He's toned down his remarks recently and the market isn't paying too much attention.' \par \par \uc2  Mahathir reiterated his belief that Malaysia wouldn't have to seek help from the International Monetary Fund to stabilize the economy. He said improved confidence could restore a comfortable degree of stability in six months. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, dealers said there was some slight \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Bank Negara, \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , very early on in the session. 'They were in very briefly,' a dealer said. 'In fact, it was hard to tell if they were there at all.' \par \par \uc2  He said corporate demand for dollars was the driving force in the market as Malaysian companies hope to defend themselves from further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deprecations. \par \par \uc2  -By James Hookway; 603 292 5254; jhookway@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 09-01-98 \par \par \uc2  0210GMT \par \par \uc2  1026 1851 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du19006fi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
