\par\par \b \uc2  Asian exotics frail but aided by  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  hope. \b0\par\par\uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 1998\par 04:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 9 (Reuters) - Asian exotic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed mostly fragile on Friday morning after recent free-falls, but hopes that central banks in the Asia-Pacific may act jointly against slides of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region supported them against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's rupiah, which had smashed below 10,000 to the dollar on Thursday, surged to a high of 7,900 following selling of dollars by some state banks, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said the rupiah was quoted at 7,750/8,500 at 0320 GMT, compared with Friday's opening rate of 10,500/11,500. \par \par \uc2  There were spreading wild rumours of riots and increased troop presence in early trade, which made trading nervous. \par \par \uc2  A U.S. bank dealer in Jakarta said: "These are all baseless rumours. The only true one was that there was a rush on basic commodities. I think Singapore players have built up extensive long dollar positions." \par \par \uc2  Thursday's plunge came after the IMF urged the Indonesian government to comply with austerity terms of the International Monetary Fund's $40 billion bailout package. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Malaysia's Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad reiterated on Friday that his country does not need financial aid from the IMF, saying Malaysia's fundamentals remained sound although its growth was expected to be at a more moderate level than in the past. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit slipped against the dollar in early trading despite continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars at around 4.7000 ringgit on Friday morning. Bank Negara had also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the ringgit on Wednesday and Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A trader at an Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk at a major Japanese bank in Tokyo said: "Trading of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly Indonesian rupiah, remained quite nervous. \par \par \uc2  "But growing hopes that nations across the region may team up to work against slides of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  checked falls of these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," she added. \par \par \uc2  Overnight dollar/yen bids were sidelined after unconfirmed rumors of quiet dollar sales by the Federal Reserve against Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  or the yen. \par \par \uc2  Market sources in Europe and New York reported that the Fed had checked rates on the Malaysian ringgit and the Indonesian rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Asked about wider cooperation in Asian nations, Larry Duke, vice president at Citibank, told Reuters Financial Television: "Sure, it's always possible. But I think it's going to be a big challenge." He added that there is a need for a change in the underlying fundamentals and aggressive policy response in the region. \par \par \uc2  Richard Samuelson, head of research at SBC Warburg Dillon Reed, told RFTV that the market still sees major risks in South Korea, including the possibility of more bank failures and weakness of the won, and there is generally an aversion to investing there. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit weakened slightly against the mark after Federal Reserve Governor Laurence Meyer said the dollar's rise was greater than expected given present economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of Australia (RBA) was rumoured to have sold U.S. dollars against the Aussie dollar. \par \par \uc2  A RBA spokesman declined to comment on the rumours. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese bank dealer said: "Concerted action from central banks in Asia, including the MAS (Monetary Authoritie of Singapore) and RBA, could be needed to prevent \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region from sliding." \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasuries edged down by midday in Tokyo as Asian operators sold holdings to raise dollars, traders said. Sales stood out in the five-year sector, putting downward pressure on overall prices, they said. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo stocks lost nearly two percent by midday as buying of blue-chip shares weakened amid worries that the Japanese government might not take new economy-boosting measures any time soon, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was down versus yen but off lows in Tokyo at midday. Its topside was capped by views that Japan may take extra economic stimulus steps in return for U.S. help in defending yen and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Yen-based gold futures on the Tokyo Commodity Exchange were lower by midday, with benchmark December down 16 yen per gram at 1,190 yen per gram. Prices fell in reaction to overnight losses in gold bullion which were triggered by a sharp slide in silver prices, traders said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du190990q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
