\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Negara Malaysia sells dollars - dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2 164 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, Jan 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early trading on Thursday, selling dollars to check a sliding ringgit, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars from 4.7000 to 4.6000 ringgit. "Negara is still in the market offering dollars," said a dealer with a European bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, Bank Negara sold dollars from 4.80 to 4.60 ringgit. \par \par \uc2  The size of Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not known, but dealers said it need not be big to move rates given the thin market liquidity. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Anwar Ibrahim on Wednesday confirmed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets "to ensure stable market conditions to allow for the efficient functioning of the market." \par \par \uc2  At 0130 GMT, the ringgit was at 4.5800/5900 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  -- Kuala Lumpur newsroom (603)201 4813; Fax (603)232 6752 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: kuala.lumpur.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du18019ce\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
