\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia Unveils Reform Plan In Effort To Avoid IMF Rescue \b0\par\par\uc2  By James Hookway and Charles Roth \par \uc2  Staff Reporters \par \uc2 900 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 1997\par 15:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR -(Dow Jones)- Battling speculation in the financial markets that Malaysia would be the next country after South Korea to go to the International Monetary Fund for a bailout, Deputy Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim Friday unveiled what many economists say is the next best thing. \par \par \uc2  "It's an IMF program without the IMF," said Simon Mahadevan Flint, an economist at Independent Economic Analysis Ltd. "They're delivering an IMF formula to restore confidence in the management of the economy." \par \par \uc2  The prescription delivered by Anwar, also Malaysia's finance minister, is wide-ranging. Abandoning what analysts said was the government's tendency to stick to rigid forecasts, Anwar set gross domestic product growth targets at 4% to 5% for 1998, a sharp revision from the 7% previously anticipated by government officials. \par \par \uc2  He said government spending will be cut by 18%, and there will be no bailout for financial institutions facing liquidity problems. The government also won't bail out corporations, although companies with temporary cash-flow problems will be helped under the new plan, Anwar said. \par \par \uc2  The package helped revive the Malaysian ringgit, which had sunk to record lows against the dollar in volatile trading earlier Friday. The dollar surged as high as 3.8400 ringgit at one point, amid fears Kuala Lumpur would shortly be knocking at the IMF's door. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell back to 3.7350 ringgit late Friday in Asia after Anwar's announcement and after some mild \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Bank Negara, \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Share prices also bot a boost from the statement, with the benchmark Composite Index rallying 5.5% to close 31.51 points higher at 607.40, buoyed by what dealers said was a more realistic analysis of Malaysia's economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  But whether Anwar's plan will survive the scrutiny of Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad and actually be implemented is still in doubt, some analysts said. Anwar and Mahathir already seem to be contradicting each other on at least one point in the plan - the decision to delay the 10 billion ringgit Landbridge project developing infrastructure links between northern Malaysia and southern Thailand. Thursday, Mahathir was reported as saying the plan would be going ahead. But Friday, Anwar said it would be delayed, and only an oil pipeline between the two countries would be constructed on schedule. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir is viewed as being keen to keep as many major infrastructure projects going as possible to meet his goal of Malaysia becoming an industrialized nation by the year 2020. Some observers are concerned he will veto Anwar's plan through the National Economic Action Council (NEAC), which was formed last week to tackle Malaysia's worsening economic problems. \par \par \uc2  Anwar tried to downplay those suggestions. He said the cabinet was fully committed to the just-announced economic plan, and that the NEAC wouldn't be able to override it. However, he couldn't confirm who would be included on the NEAC, and he didn't rule out the possibility that influential business people - who would be less keen to see major projects cancelled - could sit on the panel. \par \par \uc2  The immediate reaction to Anwar's program was overwhelmingly upbeat. "If this package can come about, then Malaysia can put its house in order. If they can trim domestic demand, they can close the current account deficit," said Daniel Lian, an economist at ANZ Investment Bank. \par \par \uc2  Closing balance-of-payment gaps is the key point in IMF programs, he said. Lian estimates that Malaysia's current account deficit stood at around 8.7% in the early part of the year but that it could fall to 5%-6% by the end of 1997, as the impact of the economic slowdown brought on by Southeast Asia's five-month-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis takes hold. Anwar said a current account deficit of 3% is achievable next year. \par \par \uc2  Despite favorable implications for Malaysia's external accounts, the economic slowdown could bring problems of its own. Lian said the deceleration could surpass Anwar's estimates and hurt the banking sector, which already is bearing the signs of liquidity problems as nonperforming loan rates rise. Although the government says nonperforming loans stand at 5.9% of total lending in the banking sector, many economists say the real figure may be higher than 10%. \par \par \uc2  As banks sell off collateral securing these loans, "a collapse in property prices could come next year," Lian said. "There could be a lot of pain in store." \par \par \uc2  It could be worse if the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  make a concerted effort to halt the ringgit's slide in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. "The only way they can relieve the slide is to hike short-term interest rates," Lian said, adding that this is something the IMF would recommend. \par \par \uc2  Anwar revealed Friday that the IMF had suggested that Malaysia increase its interest rates. But the Malaysian authorities are loath to increase rates, as it would discourage investment in the stock market and also squeeze highly leveraged corporations. A stock market that already has lost over 50% of its value since February concerns analysts, who point to commercial banks' practice of accepting stock as collateral. Anwar didn't say whether the Malaysian government would heed the IMF's advice. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1997 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020011006dtc5013mw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
