\par\par \b \uc2  S.E. Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  slide,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  lifts won. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1997\par 12:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 26 (Reuter) - Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped again on Tuesday and dealers said they could be ready for a new round of falls with the market still worried and regional bourses dropping sharply. \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, improving liquidity depressed the rupiah, propped up in recent sessions by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp rates rise last week. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said swap rates fell across the board as interest rates dropped, with overnight rupiah falling to 20 percent from 100 percent in the morning. \par \par \uc2  "There are rumours ranging from interest rate cuts to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offerings of short-term securities (SBPUs). But none of them are true," a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "What we have today is sufficient liquidity supply. I think it's resulting from maturing \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  papers which are not rolled over," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Spot rupiah was at 2,770/75 at 0945 GMT against an opening of 2,690/2,710. \par \par \uc2  A Japanese bank dealer in Jakarta said offshore players started the round of swap selling, probably in anticipation of interest rates easing. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won made an about-turn and ended firmer on stop-loss dollar sales sparked by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The won ended at 900.50 to the dollar, up both from Monday's 904.60 and from a historic low of 909.50 on Tuesday morning. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea's dollar sales, estimated by dealers at about $500 million, initially pushed the won up to an intraday high of 899.80. \par \par \uc2  Dealers attributed the won's morning fall to talk that this month's trade deficit would be bigger than expected. They said the won was likely to remain firmer for the time being due to fears of more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ringgit fell against the U.S. dollar on talk that U.S. funds were accumulating long dollar/ringgit positions in the forward market. \par \par \uc2  "We've been hearing that U.S. funds have been buying dollar/ringgit heavily in the forward market, especially in the six-months and one-year," the U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit was quoted at 2.7820/70 to the dollar after briefly falling through 2.79 against 2.7700/50 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit fell to new lows against the Singapore dollar on follow-through sales after the cross breached stop-loss limits overnight. Ringgit/Sing was at 0.5359/76 at 0945 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "There were still some long positions on the cross so once we broke 0.5450 and 0.5400, there was more selling. But fundamentally, I don't see any reasons why we should be selling it," the U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar briefly fell through 1.5000 to the U.S. dollar in line with the ringgit's drop. \par \par \uc2  It was quoted at 1.4935/55, little changed from its noon levels, after firming to 1.4895 earlier on sales of an estimated $50 million by a Tokyo party, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was subdued as domestic demand for the dollar subsided, but dealers said sentiment remained weak on lingering concerns about Thailand's economic and political outlook. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso weakened as foreign funds pulled out of the badly bruised stock market. The peso ended at 30.02 to the dollar against Monday's 29.895 close while the key stock market index finished at a new 21-month low of 2,300.82. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its rediscount rate for September to 13.188 percent from 12.572 last month. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar ended unchanged as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintained its defence of the T$28.727 level in the face of heavy demand for the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8q00n92\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
