\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia economic growth forecast lowered. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Syed Azman \par \uc2 417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1997\par 09:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, Aug 12 (Reuter) - Economists have lowered their forecast for Malaysia's economic growth this year and in 1998 due to slower-than-expected export growth and high interest rates, a Reuters poll showed on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In the poll, 12 research houses in Malaysia and Singapore forecast an average 7.9 percent gross domestic product (GDP) growth in 1997 against the 8.0 percent they projected at the end of last year. \par \par \uc2  The government has forecast a growth of 7.8-8.2 percent after reporting rise of 8.2 percent in 1996. \par \par \uc2  The economists forecast the 1998 GDP growth at an average 7.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  The houses polled were Crosby Research, J.M. Sassoon & Co, Jupiter Securities, Kleinwort Benson, Merrill Lynch, MMS International, Natwest Markets, OSK Research, Phileo Allied Securities, TA Securities, UBS Securities and Vickers Ballas. \par \par \uc2  Their forecasts for the current year ranged between 7.5 and 8.2 percent and for 1998 between 5.4 and 8.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  Economists said they had also mostly revised downward their growth projections after taking into account the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit has fallen more than 11 percent against the dollar to a 42-month low of 2.7930 on Tuesday over the past month due to heavy selling by offshore funds. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit was around 2.7700 to a dollar at 0700 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "Most people are knocking down their numbers after recent developments," said Kevin Chew, economist at Caspian Securities. "The ringgit is weakening and the period of high interest rates is longer than what we have anticipated. This will curb domestic demand." \par \par \uc2  Heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June to defend the ringgit mopped up large funds from the banking system, pushing interbank rates sharply higher. \par \par \uc2  Slower exports and foreign investment had also prompted economists to lower their forecast. \par \par \uc2  Malaysian exports in the first six months of this year rose by only 1.9 percent, to 98.4 billion ringgit against the same period of 1996. \par \par \uc2  The slower export growth in part contributed to Malaysia's unexpectedly large trade deficit of 2.8 billion ringgit for June. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia's approvals of foreign investment slumped to 5.41 billion ringgit in the first half of this year, against 17.06 billion ringgit for the whole of 1996, government figures showed. \par \par \uc2  -- Kuala Lumpur Newsroom (603)201 4813; Fax (603)232 6752 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: kuala.lumpur.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8c01ea3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
