\par\par \b \uc2  POLL-MALAYSIA 1997 GDP GROWTH SEEN AT 7.9 PCT. \b0\par\par\uc2 317 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, Aug 12 (Reuter) - Malaysia's economic growth is expected to slow down further this year and in 1998 due to slower exports and lower investment inflows, economists said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In a poll by Reuters, 12 research houses forecast an average of 7.9 percent for gross domestic product (GDP) growth in 1997 against the government's forecast of 7.8-8.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  They forecast 1998 GDP growth at an average 7.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia reported GDP growth of 8.2 percent in 1996. \par \par \uc2  Economists said they had mostly revised down their growth projections after taking into account the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil and high domestic interest rate environment. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit has fallen more than 11 percent against the dollar over the past month due to heavy selling by offshore funds. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a 42-month low of 2.7930 against the dollar on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June to defend the ringgit, mopped up large funds from the banking system, pushing interbank rates sharply higher. \par \par \uc2  Malaysian exports in the first six months of this year rose by only 1.9 percent, to 98.4 billion ringgit against the same period of 1996. \par \par \uc2  GDP FORECAST (percentage growth): \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Company\tab \tab \tab \tab  1997\tab    1998\par  Crosby Research\tab \tab \tab 8.0\tab     5.4\par  J.M. Sassoon & Co\tab \tab     8.3\tab     8.0\par  Jupiter Securities\tab \tab    7.8\tab     7.9\par  Kleinwort 