\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysian PM lashes out at  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  speculators. \b0\par\par\uc2 354 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 1997\par 05:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, July 24 (Reuter) - Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad on Thursday launched a bitter attack on "rogue speculators" whom he accused of undermining Southeast Asian economies by destabilising their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Opening the 30th foreign ministerial meetings of the Association of South East Asian Nations (ASEAN), Mahathir said: "We see a well-planned effort to undermine the economies of all the ASEAN countries by destabilising their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Our economic fundamentals are good, yet anyone with a few billion dollars can destroy all the progress that we have made," he said. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia and neighbouring countries have seen attacks on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past month, forcing devaluations in Thailand and the Philippines and expensive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts in \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped to new lows as speculators and corporates continued to pile into the dollar, triggering stop-loss orders. No signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were yet detected on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht dropped to a low of 32.63/dollar onshore, the Singapore dollar fell below the psychological level of 1.4700 on stop-loss sales while the Malaysian ringgit breached the 2.65 barrier in early trade on spillover weakness from Thailand and Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah slipped back through the 2,600 support level on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir, known for his sharp-tongued criticisms of the West, said the countries of the region were told they must open up their markets and that trade and commerce must be totally free. \par \par \uc2  "Free for whom? For rogue speculators. For anarchists wanting to destroy weak countries in their crusade for open societies, to force us to submit to the dictatorship of international manipulators," Mahathir told the ASEAN gathering. \par \par \uc2  "We want to embrace borderlessness but we still need to protect ourselves from self serving rogues and international brigandage," he said. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir did not mention any speculators by name in his speech. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia is the current chairman of ASEAN, which was expanded on Wednesday to nine members with the inclusion of Laos and Burma. Its other members are Thailand, Vietnam, the Philippines, Indonesia, Brunei and Singapore. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt7o0244i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
