\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dollar Higher Late On Liquidity Squeeze \b0\par\par\uc2 490 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 1997\par 11:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (AP-Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar is higher against the U.S. dollar in late trading Monday afternoon compared with its level two hours ago. The Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also higher than its level in early morning trading on a liquidity squeeze in the swaps market which has forced short term interest rates up to their highest levels in a year. \par \par \uc2  At 0915 GMT (5:15 p.m. EDT) the U.S. dollar is quoted at S$1.4615, a touch lower than its level in early afternoon trading and below S$1.4638 early Monday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar initially fell during morning trading as domestic market participants running substantial long U.S. dollar positions found sourcing Singapore dollar liquidity - to allow them further to short the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - increasingly difficult. \par \par \uc2  'Everyone was on the same side of the market. With everyone short of Sing' dollars, financing the positions was becoming impossible,' said the chief regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a major Asian bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Under the squeeze, overnight rates on Singapore dollar deposits reached 7%-7.5%, from 3.625%-3.75% Friday. \par \par \uc2  The liquidity squeeze had the effect of capping the dollar's rise against Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has seen the U.S. dollar appreciate by nearly 2.5% over the previous month. \par \par \uc2  'With interest rates this high, we are likely to see the dollar/Sing' rate capped at S$1.4680 for this week,' predicted a trader at a leading U.S. commercial bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was also boosted in early afternoon trading by the release of better-than-expected trade figures. Singapore's non-oil domestic exports rose 8.8% in June from a year ago, besting economists' average forecast of a 7.0% improvement. \par \par \uc2  The effect was relatively short-lived, however, as the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened when, as reported, the Singapore Trade Development Board said the government continues to monitor the Singapore dollar's real exchange rate, not its nominal rate against the U.S. dollar alone. \par \par \uc2  This was taken by traders as an indication that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singpore would allow the Singapore dollar to continue to weaken against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again weakened before being capped at S$1.4670 on lack of liquidity. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit is currently quoted at S$0.5592, up from S$0.5550 as the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has strengthened against the U.S. dollar on what traders believed to be earlier \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Bank Negara at S$2.6400. \par \par \uc2  In the local money market the overnight rate on Singapore dollar deposits is off its mid-afternoon highs at 4.00%-4.25% little changed on 4.00%-4.50% early Monday. Three-month interbank rates are at 4.125%-4.375% from 4.25%-4.375% earlier. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 21-07-97 \par \par \uc2  0940GMT \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt7l05awy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
