\par\par \b \uc2  Speculators seen targetting Malaysian ringgit next. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Syed Azman \par \uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 1997\par 10:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, July 11 (Reuter) - The Malaysian ringgit is likely to be the next target of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators after they successfully forced two central banks in the region to devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  But they said Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would put up a tough fight. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara has itself warned would be aggressive in its efforts to stamp out speculation in the ringgit. It is backed by economic fundamentals widely regarded as stronger than most other countries in the region. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the failure of the Thai and Philippine central banks to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could lead to greater pressures on the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday it would allow the peso to trade in a wider range against the U.S. dollar, with immediate effect. \par \par \uc2  Dealers saw the move as the second de facto devaluation of an Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in less than two weeks. The move came nine days after Thailand's decision to stage a managed float float of the baht. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine move sent Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  into a tailspin on Friday. At 0600 GMT, the ringgit was quoted at 2.5020/30 to a dollar after opening around 2.4930. \par \par \uc2  "The bet is on that the ringgit will be the next \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall prey to speculators," said Sani Hamid, market analyst at research house MMS International in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "But Bank Negara will do all it can to defend the ringgit. They can also impose capital controls as a last resort," he added. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara governor Ahmad Mohamed Don on Wednesday warned of tough steps, including increasing interest rates, to curb speculation in the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, spending an estimated $1.0 billion to help the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  Ahmad said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was warranted in the interest of long-term stability, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sufficient reserves to fund such \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said speculators are betting that Bank Negara will not be able to sustain its dollar/ringgit \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as reserves are drawn down. But some believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sufficient reserves to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara's reserves at the end of June stood at 70.71 billion ringgit ($28.3 billion). \par \par \uc2  "That provides substantially more ammunition than is available to the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  which had about $11.5 billion at the beginning of the pressure, and was down to about $10 billion yesterday," said P.K. Basu, director of regional economics at UBS Securities. \par \par \uc2  "I don't see the ringgit succumbing to the pressures emanating from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said speculators are also betting that Bank Negara would not revert to capital controls as this would prove a major setback to Kuala Lumpur's goal of becoming an internationally recognised capital market. \par \par \uc2  In 1994, faced with a strong flow of hot money into the country, Bank Negara imposed negative interest rates on foreign deposits, banned swaps and stopped foreigners from buying Malaysian government paper. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a ringgit devaluation was also unlikely. \par \par \uc2  "I don't see the real justification for it," said Nathan Balakrishnan, general manager at SBB Unit Trust Management. "Some are trying to short the ringgit because they think sentiment might affect the ringgit, but not fundamentally. Fundamentally I think we are better than Thailand and the Philippines," he said. \par \par \uc2  Unlike Thailand and Philippines, Malaysia's current account deficit is funded by long-term inflows. \par \par \uc2  In addition, Malaysia is the only economy in the region which has been able to maintain a strong economic growth, having posted growth of more than eight percent a year in the past nine years. \par \par \uc2  Unlike the baht and peso, the ringgit has been a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1971. \par \par \uc2  "I would emphasise that Malaysia has always had a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime ... over the past one year, the ringgit has fluctuated about 2.5 percent above and below the dollar. In the past 10 years, it has moved 10 percent either side," said Basu. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt7b02nqf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
