\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia \highlight\uc2 's Bank Negara Said  \highlight22\uc2 Intervening \highlight\uc2  To Support Ringgit \b0\par\par\uc2 135 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 1997\par 04:05\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR (AP-Dow Jones)--Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  early Wednesday to support the ringgit after the dollar hit the psychological 2.5250 ringgit level shortly after 0800 GMT, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The move followed news that Thailand will abandon its fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime and adopt a 'managed float' for the baht. \par \par \uc2  A dealer in Malaysia said market participants had tested the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  by pushing up the dollar to 2.5250 ringgit, and Bank Negara had come in to sell dollars shortly thereafter. \par \par \uc2  After that, the ringgit strengthened, with the dollar sharply lower at 2.5223 ringgit. \par \par \uc2  But dealers remained unsure as to what effect Wednesday's news from Thailand would have on the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 02-07-97 \par \par \uc2  0205GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt7204uly\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
