\par\par \b \uc2  Other Asians exposed if Thais forced to devalue. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Phil Smith \par \uc2 590 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 20 (Reuter) - If the political and economic turmoil in Thailand does leads to a devalution of the baht it would have regionwide repercussions, with Malaysia the first in the speculators' firing line, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Many analysts said they thought Thailand would not devalue and that such a move would only come if it is forced to politically following the resignation of its finance and commerce ministers this week. \par \par \uc2  So far the government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have held their firm \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  line. But what if a devaluation does come? \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian authorites are aware the ringgit is at risk again and last week top Bank Negara officials met local and locally-incorported foreign banks over \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  activities. \par \par \uc2  Analysts believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was whipping up support should the ringgit come under full-scale attack. News reports said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had warned local banks not to lend money offshore for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. \par \par \uc2  "If baht jitters continue there could well be some more slippage of the ringgit, but after last week's meetings it's doubtful anyone will take a real pop at it," said one Singapore based analyst. "Bank Negara usually means business and things could get dangerous if you were short at the wrong time." \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso is another possible target for selling and has indeed been hurt by the baht crisis, but like Bank Negara, Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas has already moved to stave off further pressure. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its overnight borrowing rate to 15 percent from 12.75 percent after the peso slid as the baht came under pressure following the departure of Thai finance minister Amnuay Viravan. \par \par \uc2  Last month, it raised rates to a two-year high of 20 percent when the baht's weakness pressured the peso. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah also stands out as a speculative target and has already suffered amid fears by offshore investors that Thailand's problems could spill over into Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Malaysia would be the first target because as well as some similar economic background factors, it also has a liberal enough exchange rate regime to allow speculators the liquidity they need to sell short. \par \par \uc2  "They do have some comparable economic figures," said Chiang Yao Chye, economist at CIBC Wood Gundy. "Speculators have been looking at Malaysia for some time now and the risk is obviously there if Thailand goes." \par \par \uc2  It was the ringgit which suffered most during the speculative attack on the baht in mid-May, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively. \par \par \uc2  But the spirit of regional cooperation seems to be alive and well and analysts said speculators will remain wary of aggressive selling, particularly those which were stung by the Bank of Thailand's aggressive moves during the baht crisis. \par \par \uc2  Singapore Finance Minister Richard Hu said on Thursday that Singapore would continue to help Thailand defend the baht against speculators. \par \par \uc2  "It's all very well looking at these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and seeing which is the most vulnerable, but after what Richard Hu said yesterday you have to be careful before taking a big position," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a leading European bank. \par \par \uc2  "You would certainly see the likes of the ringgit take a hit if we get a Thai devaluation, but you could get hit by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from every side...they've acted in concert before they could well do it again." \par \par \uc2  Singapore Newsroom +65-870-3091 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt6k03ueo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
