\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia/Stock Mkt -2: Investors Leaving Southeast Asia \b0\par\par\uc2 339 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 1997\par 13:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  As Thailand's economy has slowed, so has demand for property, leading to defaults on property loans that have crippled some banks. Analysts say foreign investors who missed the warning signs in Thailand don't want to make the same mistake in Malaysia. \par \par \uc2  'The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move focused people's attention on potential problem areas,' said Simon Woods, senior investment adviser at Caspian Securities (Hong Kong) Ltd. 'That has sparked negative vibes toward Malaysia.' \par \par \uc2  So, after Thai share prices plunged Wednesday, following an attack on the baht that sent shudders through other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, analysts said four major foreign funds decided to sell off large holdings in Malaysia's market Thursday morning. \par \par \uc2  'They're just getting rid of them. They're just doing it at whatever price they can,' said Jonathan Duckett, a member of the institutional sales team at Mohaiyani Research Sdn. Bhd. 'They just want to get out of Southeast Asia.' \par \par \uc2  Although economists and analysts insist the possibility of a financial crisis in Malaysia on the order of Thailand's is still unlikely for now, they believe investors don't want to take any chances. \par \par \uc2  'It makes people nervous,' said an analyst at a foreign brokerage house, who declined to be identified. 'You don't trust your instincts.' \par \par \uc2  Added Alex Tan, head of research at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell: 'Foreign investors are becoming risk-averse to the market. Now people are starting to run first, before they assess any measures.' \par \par \uc2  Rumors that Thailand would devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have also shaken investors in the Malaysian market who fear such a move would spook foreign investors out of the region even faster. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened Wednesday, as participants in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moved into safer \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the U.S. and Singapore dollars. But dealers said Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  then \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday to keep the ringgit from weakening beyond 2.5248 ringgit per U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 15-05-97 \par \par \uc2  1136GMT \par \par \uc2  1141 62551 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt5f03gq2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
