\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia Ringgit/Late -2: 'Spillover Effect': Dealer \b0\par\par\uc2 389 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 1997\par 12:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said there had been a 'spillover effect' Wednesday, with\par players buying dollars following market rumors of a possible\par devaluation of the Thai baht.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Singapore-based dealer said although the rumored devaluation didn't\par directly involve the ringgit, players had reacted by selling ringgit,\par and other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , such as the Indonesian rupiah, and buying U.S.\par dollars.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Dealers said players had bought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and to a lesser extent the Singapore dollar, because they are regarded as 'safe haven' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A dealer from a local bank, said the fact investors continued Wednesday to desert Malaysia's stock market also meant there was little buying interest in the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  'Everyone is running away from our market,' said the dealer. The Composite Index of 100 blue-chip stocks closed Wednesday, down 12.06 points, at 1072.49 points. \par \par \uc2  Ringgit dealers were divided as to whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakening further late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said they thought Bank Negara may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling some dollars at around the 2.5248 ringgit level, to stop the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling below the 2.5250 ringgit 'psychological level.' \par \par \uc2  But a locally-based dealer said he didn't think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  came in, because the 'issue Wednesday was Thailand not Malaysia.' \par \par \uc2  The dealer from the local bank said that while Bank Negara may have 'tested the level around 2.5248,' he didn't think it involved much dollar selling, because there had been so much dollar buying throughout Wednesday trading. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara didn't openly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, and doesn't comment on rumors of such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar could start Thursday trading, at the 2.5230 ringgit level if some players sell dollars in later overseas trading to capitalize on its gains. \par \par \uc2  In the money market, at the late morning fixing, the one-month Kuala Lumpur Interbank Offered Rates (Klibor) were at 7.30%, unchanged from Tuesday. The three-month Klibor were at also unchanged at 7.36%, while the six-month Klibor remained the same as Tuesday's figure of 7.40% \par \par \uc2  The overnight rate, which indicates short-term liquidity conditions, closed at 7.6%, up from 6.0% Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 14-05-97 \par \par \uc2  1027GMT \par \par \uc2  1026 1851 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt5e03fyd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
